  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Closing the Loop of Sustainable Procurement in Displacement Setting Using Innovative Finance</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project recognises that there is currently no waste management service model supported by the humanitarian system to close the loop on sustainable procurement from purchase to end of life of products. It aims to address this gap by exploring the capacity of using a transparent, cost effective and efficient innovative financing model to subsidize local waste management services that can be procured by UN agencies alongside products, ensuring humanitarian organizations are responsible for the waste they generate as part of their commitment to sustainable procurement and sustainable development at their end of life. The use of an innovative finance model is expected to strengthen local businesses, enhance job creation and stimulate markets, whilst also supporting more responsible waste business practices by strengthening them to collaborate with humanitarian structures, in line with Grand Bargain commitments and the localization agenda. In addition, the project looks at how innovative finance can be used to motivate individual behaviour change to repair and recycle products at approved service locations, whereby beneficiaries are incentivised to turn in waste by for example, receiving vouchers/credits/cash for their waste.</narrative>
  </description>
